{"builder":"Goal: when a config file holds malformed JSON, the error must name the file path and the line and column of the mistake. The app must still exit 1 with a `bad config:` line on stderr, and valid configs must load exactly as they do now.\n\nCurrent code:\n- `src/config.js` `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`.\n- `src/app.js` merges the result over DEFAULTS. On any error it writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exits 1. Otherwise it prints `listening on <port>`.\n- `test/config.test.js` has the tests 'reads a config' and 'bad JSON throws' (the second asserts a SyntaxError). `npm test` runs `node --test`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, read the file text first, then call `JSON.parse` inside a try/catch that catches only the parse error. Do not wrap or change errors from `readFileSync` (a missing file, ENOENT, must stay as it is).\n2. On a parse error, throw a new error. Do not return `{}`, the defaults or any other value. Its `message` must contain the path exactly as it was passed to `loadConfig` (do not resolve it to an absolute path), plus the line and column of the mistake. A good shape is `<path>: line <L> column <C>: <original message>` or `<path>:<L>:<C>: <original message>`. Keep the error a `SyntaxError` (for example `new SyntaxError(msg, { cause: err })`) so the existing 'bad JSON throws' test still passes.\n3. Work out the line and column yourself instead of relying only on Node's wording, which differs between Node versions:\n   - If the original message contains `line N column M`, use those numbers.\n   - Otherwise take the character offset from `position N` in the message. Count the newlines in `text.slice(0, N)` to get the 1-based line. The 1-based column is N minus the index just after the last newline before N, plus 1.\n   - If there is no position (for example on unexpected end of input), use the end of the text.\n   Check the result against this case: for the text `{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n`, Node v22 reports position 20, which must give line 3 column 3.\n4. Leave `src/app.js`'s behaviour as it is: it writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exits 1, and it never prints `listening on` when loading fails. Change it only if needed to keep that output.\n5. Add tests in `test/config.test.js`. Each new test must fail against the current `src/config.js`:\n   - `loadConfig` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws, and `error.message` includes that file's path.\n   - `loadConfig` on the four-line file `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}` throws with a message that includes the path, line 3 and column 3.\n   - Optional: spawn `node src/app.js <file>` on that four-line file and assert exit code 1, empty stdout, and stderr containing `bad config:`, the path, and line 3 column 3.\n   Keep the existing tests passing, including 'reads a config' (which returns `{ port: 9000 }`).\n6. Optional: update the README line to say that the message names the file and the location.\n7. Check by hand on Node v22:\n   - `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json` must exit 1, print nothing on stdout, and print `bad config:` with `$d/c.json` and line 3 / column 3 on stderr.\n   - `printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json` must exit 0 and print `listening on 9000`.\n   - `npm test` must pass.\n\nOut of scope: making `loadConfig` swallow errors or return defaults, changing how a missing file is reported, validating config values, and supporting comments or JSON5.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head, from the repository root, on the repo's Node version (v22). Use a fresh temp dir for each run: `d=$(mktemp -d)`.\n\nCheck 1. Expect: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \\\"port\\\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \\\"x\\\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; wc -c < $d/out; cat $d/out`\nPASS: the exit code is 1 and `$d/out` is empty (0 bytes).\nFAIL: any other exit code, or any stdout output (including a `listening on` line).\n\nCheck 2. Expect: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nRun: in the same setup as Check 1, `cat $d/err`, then `grep -F 'bad config:' $d/err` and `grep -F \"$d/c.json\" $d/err`.\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:`, contains the exact string `$d/c.json` as passed, and gives line 3 and column 3 as the mistake's location (for example `line 3 column 3`, `3:3`, or `line 3, col 3`).\nFAIL: any of these is missing, the path is altered (for example resolved differently or shortened), or the line/column shown is not 3 and 3.\n\nCheck 3. Expect: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"`\nPASS: it prints `OK`.\nFAIL: it prints `RETURNED ...` or `NO PATH: ...`, or it errors in some other way.\n\nCheck 4. Expect: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\\\"port\\\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; const v = loadConfig('$d/ok.json'); assert.deepEqual(v, { port: 9000 }); console.log('OK', JSON.stringify(v))\"`\nPASS: stdout is `listening on 9000`, the exit code is 0, and the second command prints `OK {\"port\":9000}`.\nFAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 5. Expect: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nRun:\n- `npm test` at the PR head.\n- Find the merge base (`git merge-base HEAD <PR target>`). Keep the PR's `test/` files but restore the base's source with `git checkout <merge-base> -- src`, run `npm test`, then restore with `git checkout HEAD -- src`.\nPASS: `npm test` passes at the head. With base `src/`, at least one test fails, and that failing test asserts that the error from malformed config JSON contains the file path (read the test to confirm).\nFAIL: `npm test` fails at the head, or no test fails against base `src/`, or none of the failing tests checks for the file path in the error.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- Read `src/config.js` to see whether the line/column comes only from Node's message text or is computed from the position or text. Computing it is more robust across Node versions.\n- Confirm that `loadConfig` on a missing file still makes the app exit 1 with `bad config:`.\n- Confirm that no code path returns `{}` or the defaults for malformed JSON."}